From 3c2040ce10421387e3a75d75d88ede8e567b5a25 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ra=C3=BAl=20=C3=81lvarez?= <raulwii2@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 21:00:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 54/56] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, let the application choose the
 extent
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 5 (present modes and scaling)
Why: the surface published minImageExtent == currentExtent ==
 maxImageExtent and NVK set force_swapchain_to_currentExtent, so every
 swapchain rendered at the layer's own size and the common WSI clamped
 any other request back to it. That was true of what the backend could
 do at the time and is not a property of the platform: every buffer
 registered on an NWindow must match the *window*, and this backend is
 what sets the window — nwindowSetDimensions, at registration, after
 nwindowReleaseBuffers and before the first nwindowConfigureBuffer,
 which is the only moment it is allowed. The compositor then scales the
 result onto the layer through the scalingMode every queue already
 carries.
 .
 On GM20B that is the difference between a frame that fits in the budget
 and one that does not: rendering at half the output each way is a
 quarter of the pixels, and 720p on a docked 1080p layer is very nearly
 exactly this.
 .
 THE SUBOPTIMAL TEST HAD TO CHANGE WITH IT, and getting that wrong is
 the whole risk. `reported` comes from bqQueueBuffer's output, which
 carries the queue's default buffer size — and two parties set that: the
 producer through nwindowSetDimensions, which is this backend, and the
 consumer when the display mode changes. While every swapchain matched
 the layer those were the same number. They are not any more, and
 comparing the application's chosen extent against the layer called
 every frame of a deliberately-smaller swapchain suboptimal. An
 application that recreates on SUBOPTIMAL would then loop until it gave
 up its own choice — the loop with no exit patch 0040 removed, arrived
 at from the other direction.
 .
 So: ignore our own echo, and compare against the output the swapchain
 was created for. The native cases are bit-for-bit the behaviour 0040
 landed.
Evidence: RUN ON A CONSOLE 2026-08-24.
 .
 t_nwindow first, because a minImageExtent published without asking
 would be a promise about an unknown: 1280x720, 960x540, 640x360,
 320x180, 160x90, 64x36 and 16x16 each registered two buffers on the
 default window and presented a frame. 16x16 is the floor that ramp
 reached, not the floor that exists.
 .
 t_vk_swapchain section G, added for this: a 640x360 swapchain on a
 1280x720 layer.
 .
   note surface extent: current 1280x720, min 16x16, max 1280x720
   ok   3 images at half the layer's size: 20 of 20 frames presented
   ok   MEASURED: rendering smaller than the layer is not reported as
        suboptimal (0 of 20 frames were)
 .
 Its first run, before the echo was accounted for, reported 39
 SUBOPTIMAL over those same 20 frames, which is what the change above
 is for.
 .
 No regressions: t_vk_suboptimal 273/273, t_vk_wsi_mt 71/71,
 t_vk_present_draw 183/183, t_vk_immediate 442/442, t_vk_caps 52/52,
 t_vulkan 62/62, t_vk_swapchain 143/143.
 .
 NOT RUN, and recorded in docs/PENDING-VERIFICATION.md rather than
 implied: the display changing under a *scaled* swapchain. Nothing in
 this process can resize a VI layer, so the docked rows of the table in
 wsi_horizon_extent_changed's comment need somebody to dock the console
 while the test runs — which is what t_vk_suboptimal's section D has
 always needed.
Upstream: no — the whole file is Horizon-only, and the nvk_wsi.c half is
 one line inside a VK_USE_PLATFORM_VI_NN guard.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Fj3whqEXnojZt37wn4R2jU
---
 src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_wsi.c |  25 ++++--
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 152 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 2 files changed, 149 insertions(+), 28 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_wsi.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_wsi.c
index bb06cc62c6e..16af6deed49 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_wsi.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvk_wsi.c
@@ -55,13 +55,24 @@ nvk_init_wsi(struct nvk_physical_device *pdev)
    pdev->wsi_device.horizon.get_image_info = nvk_wsi_get_image_info;
    pdev->wsi_device.horizon.get_fence = nvk_wsi_get_fence;
 
-   /* The window's size is the compositor's to choose and every buffer
-    * registered on it must match, so the surface reports a fixed
-    * currentExtent. This makes the common WSI clamp an application's
-    * imageExtent to it rather than leaving the backend to refuse a
-    * swapchain over a difference the application could not have
-    * avoided. */
-   pdev->wsi_device.force_swapchain_to_currentExtent = true;
+   /* NOT set, and that is the whole of Fase D on this side.
+    *
+    * It used to be true, because the surface reported a fixed
+    * currentExtent and clamping was kinder than refusing a swapchain
+    * over a difference the application could not have avoided. The
+    * surface now reports a range: every buffer registered on an NWindow
+    * must match the window, but the backend is what sets the window,
+    * and the compositor scales the result onto the layer through the
+    * scalingMode every queue carries.
+    *
+    * So an application may render at 720p and present to a docked 1080p
+    * layer, which on GM20B is the difference between a frame that fits
+    * in the budget and one that does not. Clamping it back up to
+    * currentExtent would take that choice away again — silently, which
+    * is worse than not offering it.
+    *
+    * wsi_horizon re-checks the requested extent against the range it
+    * published, because with this false nothing else does. */
 #endif
 
    pdev->vk.wsi_device = &pdev->wsi_device;
diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 67b955070a8..ee7e656df48 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -120,6 +120,15 @@
 #define WSI_HORIZON_MIN_IMAGES 2
 #define WSI_HORIZON_MAX_IMAGES 4
 
+/* Smallest swapchain extent this backend offers, per side.
+ *
+ * MEASURED, not chosen: t_nwindow's extent ramp registered and presented
+ * at 16x16 on the default window, 2026-08-24. It is the smallest size
+ * that ramp reached, so it is a floor that was tested rather than a
+ * floor that was found — nothing says 8x8 would fail, and nothing here
+ * claims it would work. */
+#define WSI_HORIZON_MIN_EXTENT 16
+
 /* NATIVE_WINDOW_MIN_UNDEQUEUED_BUFFERS. libnx's display/types.h
  * transcribes the head of Android's query enum and stops before this
  * one, so the value is written out here: WIDTH=0, HEIGHT=1, FORMAT=2,
@@ -430,16 +439,35 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_capabilities(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
    caps->minImageCount = wsi_horizon_min_image_count(surface);
    caps->maxImageCount = WSI_HORIZON_MAX_IMAGES;
 
-   /* A fixed extent, and it is not a simplification: every buffer
-    * registered on an NWindow must have the window's dimensions, and
-    * nwindowSetDimensions refuses to change them while any buffer is
-    * registered. When the *consumer* changes it — docking the console —
-    * this stops matching the live swapchain's extent, which is reported
-    * as VK_SUBOPTIMAL_KHR and not as an error: see
-    * wsi_horizon_extent_changed for why that swapchain keeps
-    * presenting. */
+   /* THE APPLICATION CHOOSES, AND THE COMPOSITOR SCALES.
+    *
+    * currentExtent stays the layer's own size, because that is what it
+    * means and it is what an application that just wants native
+    * resolution will use. What is no longer true is min == current ==
+    * max: every buffer registered on an NWindow must match the *window*
+    * and nwindowSetDimensions sets the window, and this backend is what
+    * calls it — at registration, after nwindowReleaseBuffers and before
+    * the first nwindowConfigureBuffer, which is the only moment it is
+    * allowed. The size it sets is the swapchain's, and the scalingMode
+    * every queue already carries puts the result on the layer.
+    *
+    * That is what lets an application render at 720p and present to a
+    * docked 1080p layer, which on this chip is the difference between a
+    * frame that fits in the budget and one that does not.
+    *
+    * MEASURED ON A CONSOLE 2026-08-24, t_nwindow: 1280x720, 960x540,
+    * 640x360, 320x180, 160x90, 64x36 and 16x16 each registered two
+    * buffers and presented a frame, on the same window, one after
+    * another. The floor below is the smallest that ramp reached rather
+    * than the smallest that works — nothing tried 8x8.
+    *
+    * The ceiling is the layer, deliberately. Rendering larger than the
+    * output and scaling down was not measured and is not offered:
+    * supersampling is a different feature and it would be a promise
+    * about memory this backend has not asked for. */
    caps->currentExtent = extent;
-   caps->minImageExtent = extent;
+   caps->minImageExtent = (VkExtent2D) { WSI_HORIZON_MIN_EXTENT,
+                                         WSI_HORIZON_MIN_EXTENT };
    caps->maxImageExtent = extent;
 
    caps->supportedTransforms = VK_SURFACE_TRANSFORM_IDENTITY_BIT_KHR;
@@ -486,7 +514,24 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_get_capabilities2(VkIcdSurfaceBase *surface,
          break;
       }
       case VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_SURFACE_PRESENT_SCALING_CAPABILITIES_KHR: {
-         /* The extent is fixed, so there is nothing to scale. */
+         /* STILL ZERO, AND NO LONGER FOR THE REASON IT SAID.
+          *
+          * This read "the extent is fixed, so there is nothing to
+          * scale", which stopped being true when this backend started
+          * publishing a range: the compositor does scale a swapchain
+          * smaller than the layer, and that is the point of the range.
+          *
+          * What VK_EXT_surface_maintenance1 asks about is narrower —
+          * which scaling mode and which gravity the *presentation
+          * engine* applies, chosen per swapchain through
+          * VkSwapchainPresentScalingCreateInfoEXT. This backend does not
+          * offer that choice: the mode is NWindow::scaling_mode, which
+          * belongs to the window rather than to any swapchain, and what
+          * the compositor makes of it has not been looked at on a
+          * screen. Advertising a mode would be a promise about a
+          * measurement nobody has made; zero says "none of these are
+          * selectable here", which is true.
+          */
          VkSurfacePresentScalingCapabilitiesKHR *scaling = (void *)ext;
          scaling->supportedPresentScaling = 0;
          scaling->supportedPresentGravityX = 0;
@@ -707,11 +752,19 @@ struct wsi_horizon_swapchain {
    struct wsi_horizon_surface *surface;
    NWindow *window;
 
+   /* What the application asked to render at, and what every registered
+    * buffer therefore is. It need not be the size of the layer: the
+    * compositor scales, through the scalingMode every queue carries. */
    VkExtent2D extent;
    /* The size the consumer last said this window is. Updated from every
-    * queue; a difference from `extent` is what makes this swapchain
-    * suboptimal — see wsi_horizon_extent_changed. */
+    * queue. */
    VkExtent2D reported;
+   /* And the size the consumer said it was when this swapchain was
+    * built. A difference between THESE two is what makes the swapchain
+    * suboptimal — see wsi_horizon_extent_changed. Not a difference from
+    * `extent`, which is the application's own choice and is allowed to
+    * differ from the output for as long as the application likes. */
+   VkExtent2D output_at_create;
    const struct wsi_horizon_format *format;
    uint32_t swap_interval;
    bool said_short_copy;      /* the fallback's narrowed-row warning, once */
@@ -1350,7 +1403,51 @@ wsi_horizon_extent_changed(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain)
    if (width == 0 || height == 0)
       return false;   /* nothing has reported a size yet */
 
-   return width != chain->extent.width || height != chain->extent.height;
+   /* WHAT `reported` ACTUALLY IS, and it is not one thing.
+    *
+    * It comes from bqQueueBuffer's output, which carries the queue's
+    * default buffer size. Two parties set that: the producer, through
+    * nwindowSetDimensions, which this backend calls at registration
+    * with the swapchain's own extent; and the consumer, when the
+    * display mode changes — docking takes the layer to 1920x1080, and
+    * patch 0040 measured the consumer's value arriving here over a
+    * producer that had set 1280x720.
+    *
+    * So `reported` echoes OUR request until the consumer has spoken,
+    * and is the DISPLAY's size afterwards. While every swapchain had to
+    * match the layer those were the same number and the distinction did
+    * not exist. With the application choosing its own extent they are
+    * different numbers, and comparing `reported` against `extent` would
+    * call every frame of a deliberately-smaller swapchain suboptimal —
+    * an application that recreates on SUBOPTIMAL would loop until it
+    * gave up its own choice. That is the loop with no exit patch 0040
+    * removed, arrived at from the other direction, and it is what the
+    * first run of t_vk_swapchain section G measured happening:
+    * "39 SUBOPTIMAL" over 20 frames at half the layer's size.
+    *
+    * A swapchain is suboptimal when the OUTPUT has changed under it —
+    * the size the application chose was chosen for the output it was
+    * told about at creation. So: not our own echo, and not the output
+    * we were created for.
+    *
+    *   created native, undocked   reported == extent == created -> no
+    *   created native, docked     1920x1080, neither             -> yes
+    *   created scaled, undocked   reported == extent             -> no
+    *   created scaled, docked     1920x1080, neither             -> yes
+    *   created scaled, then
+    *     docked and undocked      reported == created            -> no
+    *
+    * The native rows are exactly the behaviour patch 0040 landed and
+    * t_vk_suboptimal measured; the scaled rows are the new ones, and
+    * only their undocked case has been run — nothing in this process
+    * can resize a VI layer, so the docked transitions still need
+    * somebody to dock the console while t_vk_suboptimal's section D
+    * runs, which is what that section has always needed. */
+   if (width == chain->extent.width && height == chain->extent.height)
+      return false;   /* our own request, echoed back */
+
+   return width != chain->output_at_create.width ||
+          height != chain->output_at_create.height;
 }
 
 /* Folds one completed acquire into the meter and reports the window
@@ -2316,10 +2413,17 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_create_swapchain(VkIcdSurfaceBase *icd_surface,
    if (result != VK_SUCCESS)
       return result;
 
-   if (pCreateInfo->imageExtent.width != extent.width ||
-       pCreateInfo->imageExtent.height != extent.height) {
-      /* The capabilities report a fixed currentExtent, so this is an
-       * application that ignored it. */
+   /* INSIDE THE RANGE THE CAPABILITIES PUBLISHED, which is no longer a
+    * single value. An application may render smaller than the layer and
+    * let the compositor scale; what it may not do is ask for a size the
+    * surface said it would not take. Re-checked here rather than
+    * trusted, for the same reason the format is: the common WSI clamps
+    * only when force_swapchain_to_currentExtent is set, and this
+    * backend no longer sets it. */
+   if (pCreateInfo->imageExtent.width < WSI_HORIZON_MIN_EXTENT ||
+       pCreateInfo->imageExtent.height < WSI_HORIZON_MIN_EXTENT ||
+       pCreateInfo->imageExtent.width > extent.width ||
+       pCreateInfo->imageExtent.height > extent.height) {
       return VK_ERROR_INITIALIZATION_FAILED;
    }
 
@@ -2371,11 +2475,17 @@ wsi_horizon_surface_create_swapchain(VkIcdSurfaceBase *icd_surface,
 
    chain->surface = surface;
    chain->window = (NWindow *)surface->icd.window;
-   chain->extent = extent;
-   /* Starts as the size the swapchain was built for, so the resize
-    * check compares like with like until the first queue reports one.
-    */
+   /* The APPLICATION's size, which is what every buffer will be
+    * registered at and what nwindowSetDimensions will be told. */
+   chain->extent = pCreateInfo->imageExtent;
+   /* The OUTPUT's size, twice: what the consumer says now, and what it
+    * said when this swapchain was built. The resize check compares
+    * those two, so it starts out equal and stays equal until the
+    * consumer actually changes — which is the whole point, because the
+    * application's extent is allowed to differ from the output's and
+    * must not be read as a resize. */
    chain->reported = extent;
+   chain->output_at_create = extent;
    chain->format = format;
    chain->image_count = image_count;
 
-- 
